MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m14",
  "summary": "Explain how a vehicle air-conditioning system moves heat out of the cabin, name its parts and refrigerants, and understand the controls and safety devices.",
  "outcomes": ["Explain how a refrigerant carries heat from the cabin to the outside air using evaporation and condensation.","Name the common refrigerants and oils, and explain why they must never be mixed.","Describe the compressor types, the magnetic clutch and its air gap, and electric compressors.","Explain the roles of the condenser, receiver-drier and accumulator.","Describe the orifice tube and expansion valve, the evaporator, the condensate drain and cabin filter.","Explain the pressure switches, sensors and automatic climate control that protect and control the system."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Heat always moves from warm to cool. The refrigerant absorbs heat when it evaporates and releases it when it condenses.","The four main parts: compressor, condenser, expansion device and evaporator, with a drier or accumulator.","High side: compressor outlet to the expansion device. Low side: expansion device to the compressor inlet.","Common refrigerants: R134a and R1234yf (R12 is old and banned in most places). Never mix them.","R1234yf is mildly flammable. Treat it with care.","Compressor oil must suit the refrigerant and compressor. Electric compressors use a non-conductive oil.","Replace the receiver-drier or accumulator whenever the system is opened for a major repair.","Low-pressure switch stops the compressor if the charge is low, protecting it from damage.","Never vent refrigerant to the air. Only certified technicians with recovery equipment may open the system."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m14-l1", "title": "Heat and refrigeration", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An air conditioning (A/C) system does not make cold. It moves heat from inside the cabin to the air outside. To understand how, you need two simple ideas about heat and pressure."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"ac-cycle"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Heat moves from warm to cool"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Heat always flows from a warmer thing to a cooler thing. A cold evaporator inside the cabin absorbs heat from the warm cabin air. The heat must then be moved to the outside air, which is also warm. To do this, the refrigerant has to be made hotter than the outside air, so the heat flows out."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Latent heat"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When a liquid boils into a gas, it absorbs a large amount of heat without changing temperature. This is **latent heat of evaporation**. When the gas condenses back into a liquid, it releases the same heat. A refrigerant is a fluid that boils at a very low temperature and is used to carry heat in this way."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure and boiling point"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The boiling point of a liquid depends on its pressure. At low pressure, it boils at a low temperature. At high pressure, it boils at a higher temperature. A refrigerant boils at well below zero at atmospheric pressure. In the evaporator, at low pressure, it boils at a few degrees above freezing and absorbs heat from the cabin. In the condenser, at high pressure, it condenses at a temperature that is higher than the outside air and gives out heat."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The four main steps"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["**Compression:** the compressor squeezes the low-pressure refrigerant gas into a hot, high-pressure gas.","**Condensation:** in the condenser, the hot gas gives out heat to the outside air and becomes a high-pressure liquid.","**Expansion:** the liquid passes through a small restriction (the expansion device), and its pressure and temperature fall sharply.","**Evaporation:** in the evaporator, the cold, low-pressure refrigerant absorbs heat from the cabin air, boils and becomes a low-pressure gas. The gas returns to the compressor."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"High side and low side"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **high side** runs from the compressor outlet, through the condenser, to the expansion device. The **low side** runs from the expansion device, through the evaporator, to the compressor inlet. The compressor and the expansion device divide the two sides."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Typical temperatures in a working system"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Point in the system","Typical temperature (R134a, 30 °C outside)","State"],"rows":[["Compressor outlet","About 70 to 90 °C","Hot, high-pressure gas"],["Condenser outlet","About 40 to 45 °C","Warm, high-pressure liquid"],["After the expansion device","About 0 to 5 °C","Cold mixture of liquid and vapour"],["Evaporator outlet","About 5 to 10 °C","Cold, low-pressure gas"],["Centre vent air","About 4 to 10 °C","Cooled cabin air"]],"caption":"These are rough guides. Use the manual for the vehicle."},
      {"t":"p","text":"The big temperature difference between the compressor outlet and the condenser outlet shows the system rejecting heat, and the difference between the cabin air and the vent air shows the system absorbing it. If the high-side pipe is not hot, the compressor is not pumping, and if the low-side pipe is not cold, the refrigerant is not boiling."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The compressor moves the refrigerant and creates the pressure difference. It does not make the cold. The cold comes from the refrigerant boiling in the evaporator."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Refrigerant boils at a very low temperature. If it contacts skin or eyes, it can cause frostbite and blindness. Wear eye protection and gloves whenever you work near an A/C system."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l2", "title": "Refrigerants and oils", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Different vehicles use different refrigerants, and they are not interchangeable. This lesson explains the main types, their dangers and how to handle them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common refrigerants"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Refrigerant","Notes"],"rows":[["R12","Old type used before the mid-1990s. It damages the ozone layer and has been banned in most countries. Few vehicles still use it"],["R134a","Common in vehicles from the mid-1990s to the 2010s. It does not harm the ozone layer but is a strong greenhouse gas"],["R1234yf","Newer type, in many vehicles since the 2010s. Very low global warming effect. It is mildly flammable"],["R744 (CO₂)","Used on some vehicles. Works at very high pressure and needs special equipment"]],"caption":"Common refrigerants"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ozone and global warming"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some older refrigerants release chlorine that destroys the ozone layer, which protects the Earth from the sun's ultraviolet rays. Newer refrigerants do not, but many still add to global warming if they escape into the air. This is why venting refrigerant is illegal in many countries. Refrigerant must be recovered and recycled."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Flammability"},
      {"t":"p","text":"R1234yf is classed as mildly flammable. In normal operation, it is safe, but it can ignite under certain conditions, such as in a leak near a very hot surface. Service equipment must be designed for it. Keep away from sparks and flames, and work in a well-ventilated area."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Compressor oil"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The compressor oil lubricates the compressor and travels around the system with the refrigerant. Common types are **PAG oil** (polyalkylene glycol), used with R134a and R1234yf, and **POE oil** (polyol ester). There are different viscosity grades. Use the type and grade the manufacturer specifies. **Electric compressors** in hybrid and electric vehicles use a special **non-conductive oil**, and the wrong oil can cause an electrical leak into the refrigerant circuit. PAG oil absorbs water quickly, so keep the container closed."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Never mix"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Never mix refrigerants. Fittings are designed to be different sizes to prevent this. A mixed refrigerant has unpredictable pressures, ruins the recovery equipment and cannot be recycled. Always check the label under the bonnet that shows the refrigerant type, the charge amount and the oil type."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Charge amounts and oil"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The amount of refrigerant in a vehicle is small, typically 400 to 800 grams. The correct amount matters greatly: too much and the pressure rises, too little and the cooling falls and the compressor runs without enough oil. The amount, the refrigerant and the oil are on the label under the bonnet and in the manual. A system takes oil in a measured quantity, normally about 100 to 200 millilitres in total, shared among the compressor, the other parts and the lines."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**UV dye:** a small quantity of fluorescent dye may be added to the oil to find leaks with an ultraviolet lamp. Use only dye that is suitable for the oil and the refrigerant.","**Refrigerant identification:** use the label, the service port sizes and the manual. If there is any doubt about what is in a system, test it with a refrigerant identifier before you connect the machine.","**Moisture:** refrigerant oil absorbs water from the air. Keep oil containers sealed, and leave a system open for as short a time as you can."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use a refrigerant that is not specified for the vehicle, and never add 'drop-in' or flammable hydrocarbon gas to a system. Hydrocarbon refrigerants can explode."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The label under the bonnet is the quickest source of information. It gives the refrigerant, the weight of charge and the oil. Read it before you do anything else."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l3", "title": "Refrigerant pressures, superheat and system capacity", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A refrigeration system does its work by changing the state of the refrigerant between liquid and vapour. At any given temperature, a refrigerant that is boiling or condensing has one fixed pressure. This fact is the basis of every diagnosis with gauges. This lesson shows the numbers, explains superheat and subcooling, and works out how much heat a system can move."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure and temperature go together"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a closed container with both liquid and vapour present, the pressure rises as the temperature rises. The relationship is fixed for each refrigerant. The table shows R134a. Pressures are given as gauge pressure, above the atmosphere, in bar. Absolute pressure is about 1 bar higher."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Temperature","R134a gauge pressure","Where you meet it"],"rows":[["0 °C","About 1.9 bar (28 psi)","A very cold evaporator"],["5 °C","About 2.5 bar (36 psi)","A normal evaporator in operation"],["20 °C","About 4.7 bar (68 psi)","A resting system on a cool day"],["30 °C","About 6.7 bar (97 psi)","A resting system on a warm day"],["40 °C","About 9.1 bar (132 psi)","A resting system on a hot day"],["50 °C","About 12.2 bar (177 psi)","A condenser at moderate load"],["60 °C","About 15.8 bar (229 psi)","A condenser on a hot day, in traffic"]],"caption":"R1234yf follows a similar curve, with slightly different values. Use the chart for the refrigerant you are working with."},
      {"t":"example","title":"What a static pressure tells you","steps":["A car has been parked for hours at 30 °C. Both gauges read about 6.7 bar. This matches the chart, so there is liquid and vapour in the system, and the charge is not empty.","If the gauges read only 2 bar at 30 °C, there is no liquid left. The refrigerant is a gas only, which is why the pressure is low. The charge has leaked out.","If the gauges read 6.7 bar when the engine is off, but the cooling is poor with the engine on, the fault is not an empty system. Look for a restriction, a weak compressor or an airflow problem."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Superheat and subcooling"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Refrigerant that is boiling or condensing stays at one temperature. Once all the liquid has boiled away, any further heat raises the vapour temperature above the boiling point. This rise is **superheat**. After all the vapour has condensed, further cooling takes the liquid below its condensing point. This is **subcooling**. Each shows how much liquid is in the system at that point."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Measure","How it is found","Healthy range","What a wrong value means"],"rows":[["Superheat","Temperature of the evaporator outlet pipe minus the saturation temperature at the low-side pressure","About 5 to 10 °C","High: evaporator starved or low charge. Low: flooding."],["Subcooling","Saturation temperature at the high-side pressure minus the temperature of the condenser outlet pipe","About 5 to 10 °C","Low: low charge. High: overcharge or a restriction."]]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Working out superheat","steps":["The low-side gauge reads 2.5 bar (5 °C saturation). The suction pipe at the evaporator outlet measures 13 °C with a clamp thermometer.","Superheat = 13 − 5 = 8 °C. This is in the healthy range, so the expansion valve and the evaporator are working correctly.","If the pipe read 25 °C, the superheat would be 20 °C, which is high. The evaporator is starved of refrigerant and may indicate a low charge or a restriction."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How much heat can the system move?"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The cooling effect of a system comes from the refrigerant boiling in the evaporator. Each kilogram of refrigerant that circulates takes up a quantity of heat, called the **net refrigerating effect**, which is about 150 kJ/kg for R134a in a typical car system."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Cooling capacity (kW) = refrigerant flow (kg/s) × net refrigerating effect (kJ/kg)"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Capacity and flow","steps":["A system moves 5 kW of heat. The refrigerating effect is 150 kJ/kg.","Flow = 5 ÷ 150 = 0.033 kg/s, or about 120 kg per hour.","The whole system holds under a kilogram of refrigerant, so each gram goes round many times a minute. This is why a system that is a few tens of grams low in charge may lose a lot of capacity.","The compressor uses about 2 kW to do the work. The **coefficient of performance** is the heat moved divided by the work: 5 ÷ 2 = 2.5. For each kilowatt of work, 2.5 kilowatts of heat is moved."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why the engine feels the compressor"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The compressor takes 2 to 5 kW from the engine, which is 2 to 6 per cent of the power of a typical small car at full load. At idle, the engine speed may fall slightly, and the computer raises the idle speed to compensate. A slipping belt, a seized clutch or a failing compressor takes more power and produces noise, belt squeal and a load on the engine. That is why the computer switches the compressor off at full throttle in some vehicles."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Pressure and temperature are linked. A gauge reading is a temperature reading in disguise, and the saturation chart is the key to reading it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Keep a laminated pressure-temperature chart for the refrigerants you service next to the gauges."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l4", "title": "Compressor and clutch", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The compressor is the heart of the system. It circulates the refrigerant and builds the high pressure. This lesson explains the types and how the clutch connects it to the engine."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"compressor-clutch"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of compressor"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Reciprocating (piston) compressor:** pistons in cylinders, driven by a crankshaft or a swash plate.","**Swash-plate (wobble-plate) compressor:** a tilted plate drives pistons back and forth as it turns.","**Variable-displacement compressor:** a swash plate whose angle changes to alter the stroke, so the compressor adapts to demand without cycling on and off. Many have no clutch, or are controlled by a valve.","**Scroll compressor:** two spiral shapes, one fixed and one moving, squeeze the gas. It is quiet and efficient."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Belt drive and the magnetic clutch"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On most conventional vehicles, the engine drives the compressor through a belt. The pulley spins all the time that the engine runs. A **magnetic clutch** on the front of the compressor connects the pulley to the compressor shaft when the A/C is switched on. When a coil is energised, it pulls the clutch plate against the pulley, and the compressor turns. When the coil is off, a spring moves the plate away, and the compressor stops."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Clutch air gap"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The small space between the clutch plate and the pulley, when the coil is off, is the **air gap**. It is usually a fraction of a millimetre, for example 0.4 to 0.8 mm, and is set with shims. A gap that is too big can stop the clutch pulling in, and one that is too small can make it drag. Check the manual."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Electric compressors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Hybrid and electric vehicles often use an **electric compressor** with its own motor and inverter, powered from the high-voltage system. It can run when the engine is off. It uses special oil and has high-voltage connections. Only a trained, authorised person may work on it."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Clutch will not engage","Blown fuse, bad relay, open coil, wrong air gap, low pressure switch open"],["Clutch slips","Worn clutch plate, oil contamination, wrong air gap"],["Noise from the compressor","Worn bearing, low oil, internal failure"],["Compressor locks","Internal failure, no oil. The belt may squeal or break"]],"caption":"Compressor faults"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Compressor control and cycling"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A fixed-displacement compressor cycles on and off, as the control system cools the evaporator and lets it warm. A variable-displacement compressor stays engaged and changes its output by tilting a swash plate, using a control valve fed by pressure from the low and high sides, or by an electrical signal. The cycle rate of a healthy fixed-displacement system is about once every 10 to 30 seconds in warm weather. Cycling faster than every few seconds suggests low charge or a faulty switch. A compressor that never cycles may have a sensor fault or an overcharge."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Check"],"rows":[["Clutch coil","Resistance, often about 2 to 4 Ω, and current of about 3 to 5 A at 12 V"],["Air gap","About 0.4 to 0.8 mm, set with shims"],["Clutch plate and pulley","Look for scoring, heat marks and oil"],["Compressor shaft seal","Look for oil leaks around the front of the compressor"],["Noise","Bearing whine from the pulley or the clutch, or rattle from inside the compressor"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Check the clutch by listening for a click when the A/C is switched on and watching the plate start to turn. If it does not engage, check for power at the clutch connector."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Keep hands, hair and clothing clear of the belt and clutch when the engine runs. The clutch can engage without warning."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l5", "title": "Compressor failure, contamination and repair procedure", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A compressor is the most expensive part of an air-conditioning system, and the one that is most often replaced unnecessarily or replaced too soon in the wrong way. A new compressor fitted to a contaminated system fails again quickly. This lesson explains why compressors fail, how to recognise contamination and what a proper replacement involves."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why compressors fail"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Cause","How it happens","Evidence"],"rows":[["Lack of oil","A leak removes refrigerant and the oil with it. The system is run without lubrication.","Hot, seized or noisy compressor, a dry system, burnt oil"],["Liquid slugging","Liquid refrigerant reaches the compressor and cannot be compressed. Valve plates and reeds break.","Metal debris, noise, an overcharge or a faulty expansion valve"],["Contamination","Dirt, moisture or the wrong oil gets in during repair","Sludge, acid, black oil"],["Overheating","A blocked condenser or a failed fan raises the pressure and temperature","Burnt oil smell, the clutch badly discoloured"],["Clutch or belt fault","A slipping clutch overheats. A worn bearing seizes the pulley.","Burnt clutch face, squealing belt"],["Age and wear","Seals, bearings and pistons wear out","Noise, loss of performance, leaks at the shaft seal"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading the oil"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The condition of the oil tells a lot. Drain a little from the compressor and look at it."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Oil appearance","Meaning"],"rows":[["Clear, light colour","Healthy"],["Dark and burnt-smelling","Overheating, or acid from moisture"],["Metallic glitter or fine grey sludge","Internal wear. Metal has been circulating."],["Foamy or milky","Contaminated by moisture or the wrong oil"],["Very low quantity","A leak, or the oil is lying in another part"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What replacing a compressor involves"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Recover the refrigerant with certified equipment, and measure what comes out.","Remove the old compressor. Drain its oil into a container, and measure the quantity, to know how much oil was in it.","Look at the oil and the inside of the compressor ports. If there are metal particles or black sludge, the system is contaminated and must be **flushed**: condenser, evaporator and lines cleaned with an approved flushing agent, or replaced where they cannot be flushed.","Replace the drier or accumulator. It holds the desiccant and filter, and it is the last chance to catch debris.","Replace the expansion valve or orifice tube. They catch debris and can be blocked by particles.","Add the right quantity of the right oil. The new compressor may contain oil already, so drain and refill it to the quantity in the manual, taking account of the oil removed from the old one and the parts replaced.","Turn the new compressor by hand about ten turns, with the lines off, so that any oil or liquid in the cylinders is cleared. A hydraulic lock can break parts.","Fit new seals and O-rings, lubricated with the correct oil, and tighten to torque.","Evacuate, charge by weight and check the system, as explained in module 15."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much oil to add?","steps":["The system's total oil quantity is 120 ml. The old compressor held 40 ml when drained, but 90 ml of oil was found in the old one and it should have had about 60 ml.","A common rule is to add to the new compressor the same quantity that you took out of the old one, if it was in the normal range, and the rest of the charge is already in the other parts.","If you also replace a condenser (about 20 ml), a drier (about 10 ml) and the evaporator (about 20 ml), add their oil quantities to the system as the maker specifies. Always follow the manual's oil balance table, because too much oil reduces cooling, and too little ruins the compressor."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fitting a clutch or a compressor"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check the air gap of a new clutch with a feeler gauge and adjust with shims, as specified.","Tighten the shaft nut to the torque in the manual. Do not use an impact wrench.","Run the compressor once the system is charged. Listen, and check the clutch engagement, the pressures and the vent temperature."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A compressor that failed from debris will fail again unless the system is cleaned and the cause is cured. Cure the cause first."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never turn a compressor by hand on a system with refrigerant, and never work on an electric compressor on an electric or hybrid vehicle unless trained for high voltage."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l6", "title": "Condenser, receiver-drier and accumulator", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"These parts reject the heat, remove moisture and store refrigerant. They have to be clean and not blocked to work properly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The condenser"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **condenser** is a radiator-like heat exchanger, mounted in front of the engine radiator. Hot, high-pressure refrigerant gas flows through its tubes, and air passing over its fins takes the heat away. The gas condenses to a liquid. It needs airflow, from the vehicle's motion or an electric fan. At low speed or idle, the **condenser fan** supplies the airflow. A blocked or dirty condenser, or a fan that does not run, raises the pressure and reduces cooling."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fan control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The fan is controlled by the engine computer or a switch that responds to refrigerant pressure or engine temperature. Many vehicles use the same fan for the radiator and the condenser. If the fan does not start when the A/C is switched on, the pressures climb and the cooling falls, especially in slow traffic."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Receiver-drier"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In systems that use an expansion valve, a **receiver-drier** sits on the high side after the condenser. It stores a little liquid refrigerant, filters out debris and contains a **desiccant**, a material that absorbs moisture. Moisture in an A/C system forms acid and ice, which damage the parts and block the expansion device."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Accumulator"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In systems that use an orifice tube, an **accumulator** is on the low side, after the evaporator. It collects any liquid refrigerant that did not boil in the evaporator, so only gas enters the compressor, and it also has a desiccant. A compressor can be broken by liquid refrigerant."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replace after opening"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The desiccant becomes saturated with moisture when the system is opened to the air, so the drier or accumulator must be **replaced whenever the system is opened** for a major repair, such as a compressor or condenser replacement. Keep the new one sealed until you are ready to fit it, and add the oil it needs."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Side","System type"],"rows":[["Receiver-drier","High side, after the condenser","Expansion valve system"],["Accumulator","Low side, after the evaporator","Orifice tube system"]],"caption":"Drier or accumulator"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common faults and what they show"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["High pressure on both sides, poor cooling","Condenser blocked outside, a failed fan, or overcharge"],["Frost on the receiver-drier or accumulator","Internal blockage, the part is restricting flow"],["A temperature drop across the drier or filter","A blocked screen or desiccant, because liquid is being restricted"],["Oil patches at a joint","A leak. Oil is carried out with the refrigerant."],["Parallel-flow condenser very dirty or corroded","Tubes are thin. Replace with a new unit if blocked by debris."]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"After a condenser or compressor replacement, add the right amount of new oil to the part or the system, as the manual specifies. If you replace the condenser, the compressor and drier are usually replaced as well when the cause was a failure that spread debris. The parts hold oil, so recover and measure what you remove."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Check the condenser fins for bent, dirty or blocked sections. A condenser that cannot reject heat is one of the commonest causes of poor cooling."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Clean the condenser with a gentle stream of water from the back toward the front, not with a high-pressure jet that bends the fins."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l7", "title": "Expansion device and evaporator", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The expansion device and the evaporator are where the cooling actually happens. This lesson explains how they work and what goes wrong with them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Orifice tube"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **orifice tube** is a short tube with a small hole (an orifice) and a screen. It is fixed, and it is placed in the liquid line. High-pressure liquid is forced through the small hole, and its pressure drops. The orifice tube does not adjust, so the system uses an accumulator to protect the compressor from liquid."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Thermostatic expansion valve (TXV)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **thermostatic expansion valve** (TXV or TEV) changes the size of its opening to match the evaporator's heat load. A sensing bulb or a sensor measures the evaporator outlet temperature, and the valve opens more if the refrigerant is too warm, and less if it is too cold. This keeps the evaporator working efficiently. A TXV system uses a receiver-drier."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"expansion-systems"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The evaporator"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **evaporator** is a small radiator in the heating, ventilation and air conditioning (HVAC) box behind the dashboard. Cold, low-pressure refrigerant boils in it and absorbs heat from the cabin air that the blower pushes across it. The moisture in the air condenses on the cold fins, and this is why A/C dries the air. This water, called **condensate**, runs out through a drain tube under the vehicle. A drip of clear water under the car on a hot day is normal."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Frosting and freezing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If the evaporator gets below 0 °C, the condensate freezes and blocks the airflow, so the cooling stops until it melts. Causes include a weak airflow, a faulty temperature sensor or cycling control, or low refrigerant on some systems."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Odours and mould"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A wet, dark evaporator box is a perfect place for mould and bacteria. A musty smell when the A/C starts is the sign. The cabin filter should be replaced regularly, the condensate drain must be clear, and an evaporator cleaning treatment may be used as the maker allows. A blocked drain can leak water into the cabin footwell."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Superheat and what the expansion valve controls"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The refrigerant leaves the evaporator as a vapour a few degrees above its boiling point. This temperature rise above the boiling point is called **superheat**. Superheat ensures that no liquid reaches the compressor. A thermostatic expansion valve keeps superheat steady, typically at 5 to 10 °C, by opening wider when the vapour is too hot (the evaporator is short of liquid) and closing when it is too cool. If the valve sticks closed, the evaporator is starved, with a high superheat, a low low-side pressure and poor cooling. If it sticks open, liquid floods back, with no superheat and a cold, frosted suction line."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Low-side reading","High-side reading","Typical sign"],"rows":[["Expansion valve stuck closed or blocked orifice","Very low, may go into vacuum","High or normal","Poor cooling, frost at the blockage"],["Expansion valve stuck open","High","Low or normal","Cold, frosted suction line, liquid flooding the compressor"],["Low charge","Low","Low","Weak cooling, bubbles at the sight glass if fitted"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Water in the front passenger footwell on a hot day often means that the condensate drain is blocked, not that there is a leak in the windscreen."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Advise customers to switch off the A/C a minute before arrival and let the fan run, to dry the evaporator and reduce odours."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l8", "title": "Heat pumps and thermal management in modern vehicles", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A petrol or diesel engine makes more heat than the cabin needs, so the heater is free. An electric vehicle has little waste heat, and every kilowatt used for heating reduces the range. The answer is the **heat pump**: the air-conditioning system, run so that it moves heat from outside into the cabin. This lesson explains how it works, what a thermal management system looks like and what it means for a technician."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The heat pump idea"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A refrigeration cycle moves heat from a cold place to a warm place. In summer it takes heat from the cabin and rejects it outside. A heat pump simply reverses the roles. It takes heat from the outside air, even when it is cold, because the refrigerant evaporates at a temperature below the air temperature, and releases it inside the cabin. Because it moves heat instead of making it, it can deliver more heat than the electricity it uses."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Why a heat pump saves range","steps":["A cabin needs 3 kW of heat. A resistive electric heater uses 3 kW of electricity to give 3 kW of heat.","A heat pump with a coefficient of performance of 2.5 gives the 3 kW of heat using 3 ÷ 2.5 = 1.2 kW of electricity.","This saves 1.8 kW. Over a one-hour journey, it saves 1.8 kWh, which is about 11 km of range for a car using 16 kWh per 100 km.","In very cold weather the performance falls, and resistive heaters help."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The parts"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Job"],"rows":[["Electric compressor","High-voltage compressor, controlled by the computer. Moves refrigerant in either mode."],["Refrigerant valves","Electrically controlled valves that direct the refrigerant through different paths"],["Outside heat exchanger","Acts as a condenser in cooling mode and an evaporator in heating mode"],["Inside condenser","Gives heat to the cabin air in heating mode"],["Evaporator","Cools and dries the cabin air in cooling mode"],["Chiller","A heat exchanger between the refrigerant and the battery coolant, to cool the battery"],["Coolant circuits and valves","Link the battery, motor, inverter and charger to the system, so heat can be moved to where it is needed"],["Thermal management controller","Decides how to use heat from the motor, battery and outside air"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Modes of operation"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Mode","What happens"],"rows":[["Cabin cooling","The system acts like a normal air conditioner"],["Cabin heating","Heat from outside air, and waste heat from the motor, is moved into the cabin"],["Battery cooling","The chiller removes heat from the battery, especially during fast charging"],["Battery heating","Heat from the motor, or a heater, warms the battery for cold weather and for charging"],["Defog and dehumidify","The evaporator dries the air and the condenser reheats it"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"R744 (carbon dioxide) systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A few vehicles use carbon dioxide as the refrigerant. It does not harm the ozone layer and has a global warming potential of 1. It works at very high pressures, with the high side at 100 to 150 bar and more, compared with 15 to 25 bar for R134a. The fittings, hoses and equipment are special. **Never connect ordinary refrigerant equipment to a CO2 system.** Only workers with the right training and tools should service them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What changes for the technician"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A scan tool is needed to read the temperatures, pressures and valve positions, and to run tests and relearns.","Diagnosis uses the same pressure-temperature ideas, but you must know which mode the system is in.","The electric compressor operates at high voltage and uses special non-conductive oil. Refer this work to trained high-voltage technicians. Use only the specified oil, which must be kept sealed.","A battery coolant circuit and a refrigerant circuit can share a chiller. A leak can let coolant and refrigerant mix.","Refrigerant must be recovered and the system pressure-tested exactly as the maker states."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A heat pump is an air-conditioner with extra valves and a clever controller. The basic rules of pressure, temperature and cleanliness still apply."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never open or work on an electric A/C compressor or its high-voltage connections unless you are trained and the system has been isolated."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l9", "title": "Controls and safety devices", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An A/C system has switches and sensors that protect it and control its output. They make the system safe and efficient, and they are a common place for faults."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure switches"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Low-pressure switch (cutout):** opens and stops the compressor if the pressure is too low, such as when the refrigerant has leaked out. This protects the compressor from running without oil, which travels with the refrigerant.","**High-pressure switch (cutout):** stops the compressor, or starts the fan, if the pressure rises too far, for example when the condenser is blocked.","**Pressure transducer:** a sensor that gives the computer a continuous pressure signal, which the computer uses to control the fan and compressor."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Temperature sensors and cycling"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **evaporator temperature sensor** (thermistor) tells the system when to switch the compressor off, to stop the evaporator freezing, and back on. This is **compressor cycling**. An **ambient temperature sensor** and a cabin temperature sensor help automatic systems to adjust."},
      {"t":"h","text":"High-pressure relief"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **pressure relief valve** on the high side opens if the pressure becomes dangerously high, releasing refrigerant to prevent a part from bursting. It resets in many designs. If one opens, find out why the pressure was so high."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Automatic climate control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An automatic climate control (ACC) system holds a set temperature. A control module reads the sensors for cabin, ambient and evaporator temperature, sunlight and sometimes humidity. It then controls the blower speed, the compressor, and the **blend doors**, which are flaps that mix hot and cold air, using small **actuators** (motors). The module also stores fault codes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The A/C request circuit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When the A/C button is pressed, the signal usually goes to the engine computer. The computer checks that the engine is running, the pressure is in range, the evaporator is not freezing and that the engine is not too hot or under heavy load, and then switches on the clutch relay. This is why a compressor may not start when a related sensor has failed."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Device","Protects against"],"rows":[["Low-pressure switch","Running with no refrigerant or oil"],["High-pressure switch","Excess pressure from a blocked condenser or overcharge"],["Evaporator sensor","Evaporator freezing"],["Relief valve","Parts bursting under extreme pressure"]],"caption":"Safety devices"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Typical switch settings (R134a)"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Switch","Opens at about","Closes again at about"],"rows":[["Low-pressure cutout","2 bar (29 psi), falling","2.3 to 2.5 bar, rising"],["Medium-pressure (fan) switch","Closes at about 15 to 19 bar to run the fan faster","Opens at about 12 to 15 bar"],["High-pressure cutout","About 30 to 32 bar (435 to 465 psi)","About 24 to 26 bar"],["Pressure relief valve","About 35 to 40 bar","Reseats at a lower pressure, or must be replaced"]],"caption":"These are approximate. Switch values differ between makers. Check the manual."},
      {"t":"p","text":"A technician can test a switch by checking continuity across its terminals when the system pressure is in the right range, and by watching the pressure at which the compressor drops out on the gauge, with a safe way of raising or lowering the pressure. A switch that is out of range protects nothing, so replace it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"If the compressor will not run, a protection device may be stopping it for a good reason. Find out why before bypassing anything."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never bypass a pressure switch to make a compressor run on a system that is low on refrigerant. The compressor can fail within seconds."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l10", "title": "A/C controls, sensors and diagnosis in depth", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Behind a simple A/C button is a network of switches, sensors and relays that protects the system and keeps the cabin comfortable. Most A/C complaints that are not caused by low refrigerant are caused by this control system. This lesson explains how the system decides to run, how automatic systems work and how to test a compressor that will not switch on."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Conditions for the compressor to run"},
      {"t":"p","text":"For the compressor clutch to be energised, many conditions must be met at once. Think of them as a chain. If any link is broken, the compressor does not run, and the fault might not be the A/C system at all."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Condition","Checked by","Typical fault"],"rows":[["Driver requests A/C","A button, or the climate control module","Worn switch, dead module, no power"],["Blower is on","The blower relay or the module","Blown fuse, bad blower resistor"],["Refrigerant pressure in range","Low and high pressure switches or a transducer","Low charge, a sticking switch, a high pressure from a blocked condenser"],["Evaporator not freezing","Evaporator temperature sensor","A bad sensor reading too cold"],["Engine conditions OK","The engine computer: speed, coolant temperature, throttle position, load","A coolant sensor fault, an overheating engine, a limp mode"],["Clutch relay and fuse","The relay is driven by the engine or climate computer","Bad relay, fuse, open clutch coil, bad earth"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Finding why the compressor will not engage"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the refrigerant pressure with the gauges. If the static pressure is very low, the low-pressure switch is protecting the compressor. Find the leak.","Start the engine, switch the A/C and blower on, and listen for the clutch click. Look at the clutch plate for movement.","If there is no click, check the fuse and relay, then measure the voltage at the clutch connector. If there is 12 V and the clutch does not engage, check the clutch's earth and its coil resistance. If there is no voltage, trace back through the relay, the pressure switches and the controls.","Use a scan tool to see the request, the pressure sensor reading, the evaporator temperature and the reason the computer gives for a refusal.","Bypass a switch with a jumper only as a short test and only when the system has a normal charge, to avoid damaging the compressor."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Automatic climate control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An automatic system measures the cabin temperature, the outside temperature, the evaporator temperature and often sunlight and humidity. It calculates what the blower speed, air temperature and air distribution should be, and drives small motors that move the **blend door**, the **mode doors** and the **recirculation door**. Many of these are **stepper motors**, and they know their position by counting steps. After a battery disconnect, or after replacing an actuator, the system may need to **calibrate** the doors with a scan tool. A door that is out of calibration blows the wrong temperature."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Sensor","What it tells the system","Fault effect"],"rows":[["In-car temperature sensor","Cabin air temperature, often with a small fan to draw air past it","Cabin gets too hot or too cold. A blocked fan or aspirator"],["Ambient temperature sensor","Outside air temperature","Wrong compensation. May read wrong after engine heat-soak."],["Evaporator temperature sensor","Evaporator surface temperature","Compressor cycles too often or not at all, or the evaporator freezes"],["Sunload sensor","Strength of sunlight through the windscreen","System does not compensate for sun"],["Humidity sensor","Moisture in the cabin air","Windows mist up"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fan control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The condenser fan must work whenever the A/C is on and the vehicle is slow. The engine computer or a separate module controls the fan, using a signal from the pressure transducer. A fan that does not start allows the high-side pressure to climb until the high-pressure switch cuts the compressor, and the A/C works on the move but fails in traffic. A fan that is too slow gives similar symptoms."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Diagnosing poor cooling in traffic","steps":["A car cools well on the motorway but warm air comes out at idle in traffic. The gauges at idle show 3.0 bar on the low side and 24 bar on the high side, higher than normal.","The static pressure is fine, so the charge is not low. A high-side pressure that is too high at idle suggests too little airflow over the condenser.","With the A/C on, the condenser fan does not run. The fan fuse and relay are good. The fan control module is not commanding the fan. A scan tool shows no signal from the pressure transducer. The transducer wiring is faulty, and repairing it restores the fan."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A compressor that will not run is usually being told not to. Find which condition in the chain is not met before you replace anything."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Print or note the reason the climate computer gives for blocking the compressor. Many scan tools show it directly."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m14-l11", "title": "Refrigerant regulations, records and the environment", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Refrigerants are controlled by law in most countries, because of what they do to the atmosphere and what they can do to a person. A technician who handles refrigerant must know the rules, keep records and use approved equipment. This lesson summarises the common requirements. Always check the law in your own country, because it differs from place to place and changes over time."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Two environmental problems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some refrigerants harm the **ozone layer**, the thin layer of gas high in the atmosphere that shields us from harmful ultraviolet rays. This is measured by the **ozone depletion potential** (ODP). Others do not harm the ozone, but trap heat when they escape, adding to **global warming**. This is measured by the **global warming potential** (GWP), compared with carbon dioxide, which is 1."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Refrigerant","ODP","GWP (100 years, approx.)","Notes"],"rows":[["R12","1.0","About 10,900","Banned or phased out in most countries"],["R134a","0","About 1,430","Being phased out in new cars in many regions"],["R1234yf","0","Less than 1","Replacing R134a. Mildly flammable."],["R744 (CO2)","0","1","High-pressure systems, special equipment"],["Hydrocarbons","0","Very low","Highly flammable. Not allowed in systems designed for other refrigerants."]]},
      {"t":"example","title":"What a gram of R134a is worth in carbon dioxide","steps":["A system leaks 100 g of R134a to the air. The GWP is 1,430, so it has the warming effect of 100 × 1,430 = 143,000 g of CO2, or 143 kg.","That is the same as burning about 60 litres of petrol (about 2.3 kg of CO2 per litre).","This is why leaking systems must be repaired, and refrigerant must be recovered, not released."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What the rules usually say"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["It is illegal to **vent** refrigerant into the air on purpose.","Only **certified** persons, with approved training, may service A/C systems, buy refrigerant in many places, or recover and recycle it.","Workshops must use **approved recovery and recycling equipment**, designed for the refrigerant in use, and keep it calibrated.","A system must be tested for leaks, and leaks repaired, before it is recharged.","**Records** must be kept: the vehicle, the refrigerant recovered, the amount added, the equipment used and the person who did the work.","Refrigerant must be stored in approved cylinders, labelled, and not left in a hot place. Empty cylinders are not thrown away but returned to the supplier.","Different refrigerants must not be mixed, and recovery machines must not be used on the wrong refrigerant."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Handling refrigerant safely"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Wear eye protection and gloves. Liquid refrigerant freezes the skin and eyes.","Work in a ventilated place. The gas is heavier than air, so it collects in pits and low areas.","Never use a naked flame near refrigerant. Heat turns it into toxic gases, including hydrofluoric acid and phosgene.","Do not smoke when you work with refrigerant.","Never heat a cylinder, and never fill a cylinder past its safe limit by weight. Overfilled cylinders can burst.","Do not use compressed air to test an A/C system. It can form an explosive mixture with the oil."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Responsibility to the customer"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Tell the customer if their system has leaked and what it will cost to repair. Explain honestly that topping up a leaking system only delays the problem, and that the refrigerant they pay for escapes. Do not sell or fit unapproved 'top-up cans' with sealants, which can block parts and contaminate recovery machines. If the system contains an unknown or illegal refrigerant, such as a hydrocarbon, refuse to connect your equipment, and explain the reason."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Recover, repair, evacuate, recharge by weight and record it. That order, and the paperwork, is the legal and professional standard."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never knowingly vent refrigerant. Never connect recovery equipment to a system containing an unknown gas without identifying it first."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Identify A/C parts and trace the refrigerant flow","objective":"Identify the main A/C components on a vehicle or demonstrator, trace the path of the refrigerant, mark the high and low sides, and check the clutch operation without opening the system.","time":"60 minutes","tools":["A vehicle or A/C demonstrator with the manual","Labels or coloured markers for the high and low sides","Digital multimeter","Safety glasses and gloves","Notepad and a component diagram"],"safety":["Do not open the refrigerant system or loosen any fittings.","Wear safety glasses and keep hands clear of the belt, clutch and fans while the engine runs.","Use only temperature touches where it is safe, since parts can be very hot or very cold.","Keep clear of the condenser fan, which may start without warning.","Run the engine only where exhaust is extracted."],"steps":["Read the A/C label under the bonnet and note the refrigerant type, the charge weight and the oil type.","Find and name the compressor, condenser, receiver-drier or accumulator, expansion device and evaporator.","Trace the pipes from the compressor outlet to the condenser, and mark the high side on your diagram.","Trace the pipes from the expansion device through the evaporator back to the compressor, and mark the low side.","Identify the type of expansion device and the drier or accumulator.","Find the pressure switches and the service ports. Note the port sizes and cap colours.","Start the engine and switch on the A/C. Listen for the clutch click and watch the plate turn.","Check that the condenser fan runs when the A/C is on.","Carefully touch the low-side pipe near the evaporator and the high-side pipe near the compressor outlet, noting the difference in temperature.","Switch off the A/C and engine.","Locate the condensate drain and note where it exits.","Complete the diagram and write a short description of the refrigerant path."],"checks":["I read the label and recorded the refrigerant, charge and oil.","I named every main component and found it on the vehicle.","I traced the high and low sides correctly.","I observed the clutch and fan operate.","I worked safely and did not open the system."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m14-q01","q":"What does an air conditioning system actually do?","options":["It makes cold air from the exhaust gas of the engine and blows it in","It moves heat from inside the cabin to the outside air","It stores cold air in the receiver during the night for later use","It cools the air by removing all of the moisture from it only"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l1","explain":"The system carries heat out using a refrigerant that evaporates and condenses."},
    {"id":"m14-q02","q":"Which component raises the pressure of the refrigerant?","options":["The expansion valve","The evaporator","The compressor","The condenser"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l1","explain":"The compressor squeezes the gas into a hot, high-pressure gas."},
    {"id":"m14-q03","q":"Where does the refrigerant absorb heat from the cabin?","options":["In the compressor, where it is squeezed","In the receiver-drier, where it is filtered","In the evaporator, where it boils","In the condenser, where it condenses"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l1","explain":"Boiling absorbs latent heat, so the evaporator cools the air."},
    {"id":"m14-q04","q":"Where does the refrigerant release its heat to the outside air?","options":["In the compressor, where it is squeezed","In the expansion valve, where it expands","In the evaporator, where it boils","In the condenser, where it condenses"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l1","explain":"The hot gas gives out heat and becomes a high-pressure liquid."},
    {"id":"m14-q05","q":"What does the expansion device do?","options":["It stores a reserve of liquid refrigerant for the evaporator to use","It raises the pressure of the gas before it reaches the condenser","It filters moisture and debris from the liquid refrigerant in the line","It drops the pressure of the liquid so that it can boil and become cold"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l1","explain":"The restriction drops the pressure and temperature sharply."},
    {"id":"m14-q06","q":"Which part divides the high side from the low side?","options":["The blower motor and the condenser fan, either side of the radiator","The compressor and the expansion device","The condenser and the evaporator, either side of the cabin blower","The drier and the accumulator, either side of the condenser coil"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l1","explain":"The high side runs from the compressor outlet to the expansion device."},
    {"id":"m14-q07","q":"Which part of a working A/C system should feel hot to the touch?","options":["The pipe leaving the evaporator","The pipe leaving the compressor","The line after the expansion device","The evaporator itself"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l1","explain":"The compressor outlet carries hot, high-pressure gas. The evaporator side is cold."},
    {"id":"m14-q08","q":"Which refrigerant is mildly flammable and used in many newer vehicles?","options":["Water, which is used in some very early cooling systems","R1234yf","R12, which is the old ozone-damaging type banned by most countries","R134a, which is used in vehicles built from the mid-1990s onwards"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l2","explain":"Equipment and procedures must be designed for it. Keep away from sparks and flames."},
    {"id":"m14-q09","q":"Why is venting refrigerant to the air not allowed in many countries?","options":["Refrigerants harm the ozone layer or add strongly to global warming","It wastes the oil in the system, which is then very costly to replace","It makes the air in the workshop too cold for the technicians to work in","It damages the paint on nearby vehicles and the floor of the bay"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l2","explain":"Refrigerant must be recovered and recycled."},
    {"id":"m14-q10","q":"Why must refrigerants never be mixed?","options":["A mixture has unpredictable pressures and cannot be recycled","The mixture is always heavier than either gas and so it sinks in the lines","The mixture makes the compressor run faster than it is designed to","The mixture changes colour in the sight glass and cannot be seen"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l2","explain":"The system fittings differ in size to prevent mixing."},
    {"id":"m14-q11","q":"What special oil do electric compressors in hybrid and electric vehicles need?","options":["A non-conductive oil specified by the maker","Any PAG oil that is on the shelf of the workshop at the time","Ordinary engine oil of the grade that suits the engine in the car","Brake fluid that is topped up through the service port by a technician"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l2","explain":"The wrong oil can leak electricity into the refrigerant circuit."},
    {"id":"m14-q12","q":"Where can you find the refrigerant type and charge weight on a vehicle?","options":["On the label under the bonnet","On the windscreen, in the lower corner on the driver's side","On the tyre placard that is fitted to the door pillar of the vehicle","In the fuel filler flap at the side of the rear wing"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l2","explain":"Read it before you start. It also gives the oil type."},
    {"id":"m14-q13","q":"A system with a label under the bonnet should be recharged with how much refrigerant?","options":["Until the pressure reaches a certain bar","Until the cooling feels cold enough","One full can, whatever the size","The weight stated on the label"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l2","explain":"The charge is set by weight. The label under the bonnet gives the refrigerant, the amount and the oil."},
    {"id":"m14-q14","q":"A car has stood at 30 °C and both gauges read about 6.7 bar. What does this show?","options":["The system is completely empty of charge","The compressor is seized and cannot turn","Liquid is present, so it holds a charge","The system is overcharged with refrigerant"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l3","explain":"At 30 °C, R134a in equilibrium has a pressure of about 6.7 bar. A normal static reading means the charge is at least partly there."},
    {"id":"m14-q15","q":"The low-side pressure of 2.5 bar gives a 5 °C saturation temperature and the suction pipe is 13 °C. What is the superheat?","options":["8 °C","18 °C","5 °C","13 °C"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l3","explain":"Superheat = pipe temperature − saturation temperature = 13 − 5 = 8 °C. This is normal."},
    {"id":"m14-q16","q":"A system moves 5 kW of heat and the compressor uses 2 kW. What is the coefficient of performance?","options":["0.4","3","10","2.5"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l3","explain":"COP = heat moved ÷ work = 5 ÷ 2 = 2.5. For each kilowatt of work, 2.5 kW of heat is moved."},
    {"id":"m14-q17","q":"High superheat at the evaporator outlet suggests what?","options":["The evaporator is flooded with liquid","The cabin filter is dirty","The evaporator is starved of liquid","The condenser fan is too fast"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l3","explain":"Superheat rises when too little liquid reaches the evaporator, because the liquid boils away early. Low charge or a restriction causes it."},
    {"id":"m14-q18","q":"What does the magnetic clutch do?","options":["It limits the pressure on the high side so that it cannot rise too far","It filters the refrigerant before the compressor takes it in at the inlet","It changes the speed of the engine to suit the load of the compressor","It connects the compressor to its pulley when the A/C is switched on"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l4","explain":"The coil pulls the plate against the pulley."},
    {"id":"m14-q19","q":"What is the clutch air gap?","options":["The gap between the compressor and the engine block where it is bolted","The space at the top of the receiver-drier above the liquid level","The distance between two of the belt ribs on the compressor pulley","The small space between the clutch plate and pulley when the coil is off"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l4","explain":"It is usually a fraction of a millimetre and is set with shims."},
    {"id":"m14-q20","q":"A compressor clutch does not engage but there is power at the coil. What might be the cause?","options":["A wrong air gap or an open coil","A blocked condensate drain that is letting water into the cabin","A blocked fin on the condenser that is raising the pressure","A dirty cabin filter that is cutting down the flow of air to the vents"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l4","explain":"The coil may be open, or the gap may be too large to pull in."},
    {"id":"m14-q21","q":"What is a variable-displacement compressor?","options":["One that pumps air instead of refrigerant through the evaporator","One that has two clutches, one on each side of the compressor","One that changes its stroke to match demand without cycling on and off","One that runs only at idle speed and is switched off at all others"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l4","explain":"Many have no clutch, and are controlled by a valve."},
    {"id":"m14-q22","q":"What is a typical clutch air gap?","options":["About 0.4 to 0.8 mm","About 4 to 8 mm","About 0.004 to 0.008 mm","About 40 to 80 mm"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l4","explain":"The air gap is a fraction of a millimetre, set with shims. Too wide and the clutch may not engage. Too narrow and it can drag."},
    {"id":"m14-q23","q":"Metal particles are found in the oil of a failed compressor. What must be done before fitting a new one?","options":["Fit the new compressor straight away","Flush it and replace drier and valve","Add extra oil to wash the particles away","Add a stop-leak sealer"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l5","explain":"Debris spreads round the circuit. If it is not removed, the new compressor will be damaged at once."},
    {"id":"m14-q24","q":"Why turn a new compressor by hand before starting it?","options":["To check that the shaft is the right colour","To fill the clutch with grease","Trapped liquid can lock a cylinder","To remove the packing paper from the pistons"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l5","explain":"A liquid cannot be compressed. Turning by hand clears any trapped liquid and prevents broken parts."},
    {"id":"m14-q25","q":"Why is the drier or accumulator replaced when a compressor is replaced?","options":["It is a part that always breaks at the same time","It holds the filter and desiccant","It controls the engine idle speed","It stores the oil for the next compressor"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l5","explain":"The drier is the system's last defence. After a failure, its desiccant and screen are contaminated and it is cheap compared with a new compressor."},
    {"id":"m14-q26","q":"What does the condenser do?","options":["It absorbs heat from the cabin air that is blown across its fins","It raises the refrigerant pressure ahead of the expansion device","It rejects heat from the hot refrigerant gas to the outside air","It stores the oil for the compressor until the system is switched on"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l6","explain":"The gas condenses to a liquid as it loses heat."},
    {"id":"m14-q27","q":"What happens if the condenser fan does not run when the A/C is on in slow traffic?","options":["Pressures fall and cooling improves","The evaporator freezes at once","The compressor runs faster","Pressures rise and cooling falls"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l6","explain":"The condenser cannot reject heat without airflow."},
    {"id":"m14-q28","q":"What does the desiccant in a receiver-drier do?","options":["It increases the pressure of the refrigerant in the liquid line","It adds oil to the refrigerant before it reaches the compressor","It absorbs moisture from the refrigerant","It cools the refrigerant before it goes through the expansion device"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l6","explain":"Moisture causes acid and ice in the system."},
    {"id":"m14-q29","q":"When must the receiver-drier or accumulator be replaced?","options":["Whenever the system has been opened for a major repair","Only if it looks dirty or damaged from the outside of the vehicle","Never, since it lasts for the whole life of the vehicle","Every time the engine oil is changed at a normal service"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l6","explain":"The desiccant saturates when it is exposed to moist air."},
    {"id":"m14-q30","q":"Which type of system uses an accumulator?","options":["A system with no compressor","An orifice tube system, on the low side","An expansion valve system, on the high side","An electric heating system"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l6","explain":"The accumulator protects the compressor from liquid refrigerant."},
    {"id":"m14-q31","q":"Frost forms on a receiver-drier. What does this suggest?","options":["A fully charged system","A worn drive belt","A weak battery","An internal blockage"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l6","explain":"A restriction makes a pressure drop and a temperature drop at the point of the blockage, which freezes moisture from the air."},
    {"id":"m14-q32","q":"What is the difference between an orifice tube and a thermostatic expansion valve?","options":["The valve stores the refrigerant for the system, but the tube does not","The orifice tube changes with temperature, but the valve stays fixed","The orifice tube has a fixed opening, while the valve changes its opening to match the load","They are exactly the same part, sold under two different names"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l7","explain":"TXV systems use a receiver-drier and orifice tube systems use an accumulator."},
    {"id":"m14-q33","q":"Water drips from under a vehicle on a hot day with the A/C on. What does this normally mean?","options":["A coolant leak from a hose or the radiator under the bonnet","Condensate from the evaporator draining normally","A refrigerant leak from a cracked pipe, which needs repair at once","An engine oil leak from the sump gasket onto the ground"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l7","explain":"The cold evaporator condenses moisture from the air, and it drains out under the car."},
    {"id":"m14-q34","q":"A musty smell comes from the vents when the A/C starts. What is the likely cause?","options":["A leaking fuel tank under the rear seat of the vehicle","Too much refrigerant in the system and a pressure that is too high","Mould or bacteria in the damp evaporator box","A failed compressor clutch that has locked on to the pulley"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l7","explain":"Replace the cabin filter, clear the drain and use an approved cleaning treatment."},
    {"id":"m14-q35","q":"What may a frozen evaporator cause?","options":["Reduced airflow and then poor cooling until it melts","A stronger smell of refrigerant in the cabin","A higher condenser temperature, because of a blockage","A louder compressor, because the oil is thick"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l7","explain":"Ice blocks the airflow through the evaporator."},
    {"id":"m14-q36","q":"Why should customers switch off the A/C a minute before arriving and let the fan run?","options":["It increases the refrigerant charge in the system for the next trip","It dries the evaporator and reduces odours","It cleans the condenser fins by drawing air through them","It lowers the engine temperature before the engine is switched off"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l7","explain":"A dry evaporator is less likely to grow mould."},
    {"id":"m14-q37","q":"An expansion valve is stuck open. What are the typical signs?","options":["Very low low-side pressure and a starved evaporator","No refrigerant in the system","A frosted suction line, flooding","A very hot suction line"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l7","explain":"A valve stuck open lets too much liquid through. The evaporator floods, superheat is lost and liquid can return to the compressor."},
    {"id":"m14-q38","q":"How does a heat pump heat a cabin?","options":["It moves outside heat into the cabin","It burns a small amount of fuel","It makes heat from a resistive element only","It takes heat only from the battery"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l8","explain":"A heat pump runs the refrigeration cycle so that it takes heat from outside and delivers it inside."},
    {"id":"m14-q39","q":"A cabin needs 3 kW of heat. A heat pump has a COP of 2.5. How much electricity does it use?","options":["3 kW","7.5 kW","2.5 kW","1.2 kW"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l8","explain":"Electricity = heat ÷ COP = 3 ÷ 2.5 = 1.2 kW. A resistive heater would use 3 kW."},
    {"id":"m14-q40","q":"Why are R744 (CO2) systems a special case for service?","options":["They run at very high pressure","They contain mineral oil only","They run at very low pressure","They are always empty of refrigerant"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l8","explain":"The high side may reach 100 to 150 bar or more. Ordinary equipment is not rated for it."},
    {"id":"m14-q41","q":"What does a low-pressure switch do?","options":["It stops the compressor when the system pressure is too low","It starts the compressor when the pressure is too low to make cold","It changes the speed of the blower motor in the cabin","It limits the pressure on the high side of the system"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l9","explain":"Low pressure can mean the refrigerant and oil have leaked out."},
    {"id":"m14-q42","q":"Why must a pressure switch never be bypassed to make a compressor run?","options":["The switch controls the air gap of the clutch and keeps it correct","The switch is needed to keep the condenser fins clean at all times","The switch is a part of the evaporator and keeps it from freezing","The compressor can fail within seconds without refrigerant and oil"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l9","explain":"The oil travels with the refrigerant, so a low charge means poor lubrication."},
    {"id":"m14-q43","q":"What is the job of the evaporator temperature sensor?","options":["To measure the engine coolant temperature for the dash gauge","To cycle the compressor and stop the evaporator freezing","To control the speed of the condenser fan on the radiator","To measure the outside air temperature for the climate control module"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l9","explain":"It switches the compressor off before ice forms."},
    {"id":"m14-q44","q":"What do blend doors do in an automatic climate control system?","options":["They filter the dust and pollen out of the air before it is blown","They block the exhaust gas from entering the cabin through the vents","They lock all of the doors of the vehicle when it is moving","They mix hot and cold air to reach the set temperature"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l9","explain":"Small motors, called actuators, move the doors on command from the control module."},
    {"id":"m14-q45","q":"What is a high-pressure relief valve for?","options":["To stop the compressor at idle speed in hot weather","To release refrigerant if the pressure becomes dangerously high","To add refrigerant to the system when the pressure becomes too low","To cool the condenser by spraying refrigerant onto its fins"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l9","explain":"It stops a part from bursting. Find out why the pressure was so high."},
    {"id":"m14-q46","q":"About what pressure does a high-pressure cutout switch on an R134a system open?","options":["About 30 to 32 bar","About 3 to 4 bar, near atmospheric","About 1 to 2 bar, near a vacuum","About 300 bar, as in a diesel rail"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l9","explain":"The high-pressure switch protects the system from excessive pressure, typically opening at around 30 to 32 bar."},
    {"id":"m14-q47","q":"A compressor will not engage. What should you check first?","options":["The tyre pressures on all four wheels","The colour of the paint on the bonnet","The wiper blades on the windscreen","Refrigerant pressure with gauges"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l10","explain":"If the charge is low, the low-pressure switch is protecting the compressor. Always check the pressure before bypassing anything."},
    {"id":"m14-q48","q":"A car cools well at speed but warm at idle. High-side pressure at idle is too high. What is likely?","options":["A completely empty refrigerant charge","Too little air over the condenser","A flat spare tyre in the boot area","A worn gearbox in the transmission"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l10","explain":"At speed the airflow is enough. At idle the fan must do it. A fan fault raises the high-side pressure."},
    {"id":"m14-q49","q":"What does a stepper motor in a climate system do?","options":["It pumps refrigerant round the system","It switches the compressor on and off","It moves a door in counted steps","It measures the cabin air humidity"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l10","explain":"Many blend and mode doors are driven by stepper motors. After a battery disconnect or a new actuator, they may need calibration."},
    {"id":"m14-q50","q":"What does the evaporator temperature sensor help to prevent?","options":["The condenser overheating","The blower motor stalling","The compressor oil leaking","The evaporator freezing"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l10","explain":"If the evaporator gets below about 0 °C it ices up. The sensor tells the system to switch the compressor off."},
    {"id":"m14-q51","q":"What does GWP stand for?","options":["General water pressure","Gas weight per pound","Global warming potential","Greatest working pressure"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m14-l11","explain":"GWP compares the warming effect of a gas with carbon dioxide, over a set number of years, usually 100."},
    {"id":"m14-q52","q":"100 g of R134a escapes. The GWP is 1,430. What is the equivalent in CO2?","options":["About 100 g","About 143 kg","About 14 g","About 14,300 kg"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l11","explain":"100 g × 1,430 = 143,000 g = 143 kg of CO2 equivalent."},
    {"id":"m14-q53","q":"Which records should be kept after servicing a system?","options":["Refrigerant, equipment and technician","The colour of the vehicle","The names of the customer's children","The fuel used on the way to the workshop"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m14-l11","explain":"Records show that the refrigerant was handled lawfully. They list the amounts, the equipment and the person responsible."},
    {"id":"m14-q54","q":"Why must you never use compressed air to leak-test an A/C system?","options":["It makes the refrigerant evaporate","It can form an explosive mixture","It lowers the system pressure","It changes the colour of the dye"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m14-l11","explain":"Air and compressor oil under pressure and heat can burn explosively. Use nitrogen for pressure tests, as the manual says."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m14-c01","front":"What does an A/C system do?","back":"It moves heat from the cabin to the outside air.","lesson":"m14-l1"},
    {"id":"m14-c02","front":"What does the compressor do?","back":"It raises the refrigerant pressure and circulates it.","lesson":"m14-l1"},
    {"id":"m14-c03","front":"Where does the refrigerant absorb heat?","back":"In the evaporator, where it boils.","lesson":"m14-l1"},
    {"id":"m14-c04","front":"Where does the refrigerant release heat?","back":"In the condenser, where it condenses.","lesson":"m14-l1"},
    {"id":"m14-c05","front":"What does the expansion device do?","back":"It drops the pressure so the liquid can boil and become cold.","lesson":"m14-l1"},
    {"id":"m14-c06","front":"What is the high side?","back":"From the compressor outlet to the expansion device.","lesson":"m14-l1"},
    {"id":"m14-c07","front":"What is R1234yf?","back":"A newer, mildly flammable refrigerant with a very low global warming effect.","lesson":"m14-l2"},
    {"id":"m14-c08","front":"Why not vent refrigerant?","back":"It harms the ozone layer or adds to global warming, and it is illegal.","lesson":"m14-l2"},
    {"id":"m14-c09","front":"What oil do electric compressors need?","back":"A non-conductive oil specified by the maker.","lesson":"m14-l2"},
    {"id":"m14-c10","front":"Where is the refrigerant label?","back":"Under the bonnet. It gives the type, charge and oil.","lesson":"m14-l2"},
    {"id":"m14-c11","front":"R134a pressure at 5 °C, 30 °C and 50 °C?","back":"About 2.5 bar, 6.7 bar and 12.2 bar (gauge).","lesson":"m14-l3"},
    {"id":"m14-c12","front":"What is superheat?","back":"The temperature of the vapour above its boiling point at the evaporator outlet. Normal is about 5 to 10 °C.","lesson":"m14-l3"},
    {"id":"m14-c13","front":"What is subcooling?","back":"The temperature of the liquid below its condensing point at the condenser outlet. Normal is about 5 to 10 °C.","lesson":"m14-l3"},
    {"id":"m14-c14","front":"What is coefficient of performance?","back":"Heat moved divided by the work used. 5 kW moved with 2 kW of work gives 2.5.","lesson":"m14-l3"},
    {"id":"m14-c15","front":"What does the magnetic clutch do?","back":"It connects the compressor to its pulley when the coil is energised.","lesson":"m14-l4"},
    {"id":"m14-c16","front":"What is the clutch air gap?","back":"The small gap between the plate and pulley with the coil off.","lesson":"m14-l4"},
    {"id":"m14-c17","front":"What is a variable-displacement compressor?","back":"One that changes stroke to match demand.","lesson":"m14-l4"},
    {"id":"m14-c18","front":"What is a normal A/C clutch air gap?","back":"About 0.4 to 0.8 mm, set with shims.","lesson":"m14-l4"},
    {"id":"m14-c19","front":"What does metal in compressor oil mean?","back":"Internal wear. The whole system must be flushed and the drier and expansion device replaced.","lesson":"m14-l5"},
    {"id":"m14-c20","front":"Why turn a new compressor by hand?","back":"To clear any liquid from the cylinders and avoid a hydraulic lock.","lesson":"m14-l5"},
    {"id":"m14-c21","front":"Name three causes of compressor failure.","back":"Lack of oil, liquid slugging, contamination, overheating and clutch or belt faults.","lesson":"m14-l5"},
    {"id":"m14-c22","front":"What does the condenser do?","back":"It rejects heat to the outside air so the gas condenses.","lesson":"m14-l6"},
    {"id":"m14-c23","front":"What does a receiver-drier do?","back":"It stores liquid, filters debris and absorbs moisture.","lesson":"m14-l6"},
    {"id":"m14-c24","front":"Where is the accumulator?","back":"On the low side after the evaporator, in orifice tube systems.","lesson":"m14-l6"},
    {"id":"m14-c25","front":"When must the drier be replaced?","back":"Whenever the system is opened for a major repair.","lesson":"m14-l6"},
    {"id":"m14-c26","front":"What is an orifice tube?","back":"A fixed restriction in the liquid line that drops the pressure.","lesson":"m14-l7"},
    {"id":"m14-c27","front":"What is a TXV?","back":"A valve that changes its opening to match the evaporator load.","lesson":"m14-l7"},
    {"id":"m14-c28","front":"What is condensate?","back":"Water from the air that condenses on the cold evaporator and drains out.","lesson":"m14-l7"},
    {"id":"m14-c29","front":"What causes a musty smell?","back":"Mould in the damp evaporator box.","lesson":"m14-l7"},
    {"id":"m14-c30","front":"Why does a heat pump save range?","back":"It moves heat instead of making it, so it gives more heat than the electricity it uses.","lesson":"m14-l8"},
    {"id":"m14-c31","front":"What is a chiller?","back":"A heat exchanger between refrigerant and battery coolant, to cool the battery.","lesson":"m14-l8"},
    {"id":"m14-c32","front":"Pressure of R744 high side?","back":"About 100 to 150 bar or more, so it needs special equipment.","lesson":"m14-l8"},
    {"id":"m14-c33","front":"What does the low-pressure switch do?","back":"It stops the compressor if pressure is too low.","lesson":"m14-l9"},
    {"id":"m14-c34","front":"What does the evaporator sensor do?","back":"It cycles the compressor to stop the evaporator freezing.","lesson":"m14-l9"},
    {"id":"m14-c35","front":"What are blend doors?","back":"Flaps that mix hot and cold air.","lesson":"m14-l9"},
    {"id":"m14-c36","front":"What does a relief valve do?","back":"It releases refrigerant if the pressure is dangerously high.","lesson":"m14-l9"},
    {"id":"m14-c37","front":"Why not bypass a pressure switch?","back":"The compressor can fail in seconds without refrigerant and oil.","lesson":"m14-l9"},
    {"id":"m14-c38","front":"Typical R134a low and high cutout pressures?","back":"Low about 2 bar. High about 30 to 32 bar. The manual gives exact values.","lesson":"m14-l9"},
    {"id":"m14-c39","front":"Conditions for the compressor to run?","back":"A request, blower on, pressure in range, evaporator not freezing, engine conditions OK and a working relay and clutch.","lesson":"m14-l10"},
    {"id":"m14-c40","front":"Why does the A/C fail in traffic but work at speed?","back":"The condenser fan is not working, so the high side pressure rises and the compressor cuts out.","lesson":"m14-l10"},
    {"id":"m14-c41","front":"What do stepper motor doors need after a battery disconnect?","back":"Calibration with a scan tool, so the module knows their position.","lesson":"m14-l10"},
    {"id":"m14-c42","front":"GWP of R12, R134a and R1234yf?","back":"About 10,900, 1,430 and less than 1.","lesson":"m14-l11"},
    {"id":"m14-c43","front":"What must be recorded after servicing?","back":"Refrigerant recovered and added, equipment used, the vehicle and the technician.","lesson":"m14-l11"},
    {"id":"m14-c44","front":"Why is venting refrigerant illegal?","back":"Some refrigerants damage the ozone layer and most add to global warming.","lesson":"m14-l11"}
  ]
});
